= Multi-Currency Expenses and Foreign Exchange

== Global Operations and Multi-Currency Reporting

Noice & Toit LLP operates across multiple currency zones. Our professionals regularly incur expenses denominated in British Pounds (GBP, £), Euros (EUR, €), United States Dollars (USD, \$), Swiss Francs (CHF), Singapore Dollars (SGD), Japanese Yen (JPY), and other world currencies.

To maintain accurate financial ledgers and fair reimbursement, all out-of-pocket expenses must ultimately be converted into the primary payroll currency of the employee's employing entity (typically EUR, USD, or GBP). This chapter outlines how exchange rates are determined, substantiated, and audited.

== Methods for Substantiating Foreign Exchange Rates

Employees may substantiate foreign currency conversion through two official methods:

=== Method 1: Credit Card Statement Substantiation (Preferred)
When an expense is charged to a corporate credit card or personal payment card, the card issuer applies a specific daily conversion rate and settles the charge in your billing currency:
- Attach the relevant card statement snippet or online banking transaction printout showing the exact settled charge amount in your home currency.
- The firm will reimburse that exact settled amount, ensuring you are neither penalized nor enriched by currency fluctuations.

#card-do("Attach card statement snippets for foreign charges")[
  When submitting receipts charged in foreign currencies, upload the corresponding card statement line showing the settled amount. This ensures you receive exact reimbursement for currency conversion.
]

=== Method 2: Official Central Bank Benchmark Reference Rates
When expenses are paid in cash, or when a card statement is unavailable at the time of report submission:
- The Expense Portal automatically calculates foreign exchange conversions using official benchmark daily fixing rates published by the European Central Bank (ECB) or the Federal Reserve System for the precise date of the transaction.
- Historical fixing rates are pulled automatically into the portal once the employee enters the transaction date, original currency code, and local gross amount.
- Non-standard conversion rates from airport currency kiosks, hotel cash desks, or private tourist currency converters will not be accepted if they deviate significantly from central bank daily fixings.

== Foreign Transaction Fees and Banking Surcharges

International travel inevitably incurs payment processing fees from card networks and banks:

#card-do("Expense mandatory foreign transaction fees")[
  Mandatory foreign transaction surcharges (typically 1.5% to 3.0%) levied by commercial credit card issuers on bona fide business purchases are fully reimbursable. Ensure the fee is visible on your card statement and enter it as a separate expense line item.
]

#card-dont("Accept Dynamic Currency Conversion (DCC) at card terminals")[
  When paying with a credit card at international merchant terminals, restaurants, or foreign ATMs, always elect to be charged in the *local merchant currency* (e.g., EUR in Germany, JPY in Tokyo, GBP in London) rather than your home billing currency.
]

=== The Dynamic Currency Conversion (DCC) Trap
Dynamic Currency Conversion is an optional merchant processing service where foreign point-of-sale terminals offer to convert the bill into your home currency on the spot. Merchant DCC exchange rates include hidden markup spreads ranging between 5% and 12% above interbank rates. Employees who accept DCC unnecessarily inflate travel costs; Finance reserves the right to recalculate reimbursement based on standard ECB benchmark rates if excessive DCC markups are identified.

== Cash Advances and Foreign Currency Purchases

- *Airport Currency Kiosks*: Avoid exchanging substantial cash at retail airport currency kiosks, which charge unfavorable exchange spreads and high minimum service fees.
- *ATM Withdrawals*: If local cash is required for small incidental expenses (such as local taxis or transit tickets in cash-preferred economies like Japan or Germany), withdraw cash from bank-affiliated ATMs using your corporate debit or credit card. Discretionary cash advance ATM operator fees exceeding €5.00 / \$6.00 are non-reimbursable.
- *Cash Tracking*: Keep an accurate log of all cash purchases. Every cash expenditure exceeding €10.00 / \$10.00 must be supported by an original itemized merchant receipt.

== Foreign Value Added Tax (VAT) Recovery

In many European and Asian jurisdictions, business expenditures (such as hotel accommodation, meals, and conference fees) include Value Added Tax (VAT), Goods and Services Tax (GST), or similar consumption taxes:
- Under international tax treaties, Noice & Toit LLP can reclaim foreign VAT paid on business expenses, generating substantial annual recoveries for the partnership.
- To enable VAT recovery, the receipt or hotel folio must explicitly show the vendor's VAT registration number, net amount, applicable tax rate (e.g., 19% MwSt in Germany, 20% VAT in the UK), and gross total.
- Submitting summary receipts that omit tax breakdowns prevents the firm from recovering statutory VAT and may result in claim delays.

== Frequently Asked Questions

*Q: What if I travel to a country with high inflation where the exchange rate changes daily?*\
A: In high-inflation economies, always use credit card statement substantiation (Method 1) so that the actual settled exchange rate on the transaction date is applied to your reimbursement.

*Q: Can I claim reimbursement for leftover foreign cash currency?*\
A: No. Unspent foreign currency bills and coins belong to the employee and cannot be "bought back" by the firm. Only actual, documented business expenditures are eligible for reimbursement.
